# compiled vector index — 264MB release artifact, distributed via corpus-supply, never committed
packages/core/data/vectors.bvi
packages/core/data/*.bvi.part
packages/core/data/*.bvi.checkpoint.json

# Built SIMD accelerators for the vector scan (`packages/core/scripts/build-native.sh`),
# rebuilt from `packages/core/native/*.c`. The scan falls back to its TypeScript
# loop when they are absent, so a checkout without them is correct, only slower.
#
# The native libraries are machine-specific and stay out. `vector-scan.wasm` is
# the exception and is committed: 3 kB, one artifact for every architecture, and
# `zig cc` reproduces it byte for byte, so it is a source-derived file rather
# than a build machine's opinion. Committing it is what puts the deployment on
# the WebAssembly tier without adding a C toolchain to the Railway build.
# The pattern excludes the directory's *contents* rather than the directory:
# git never descends into an excluded directory, so `dist-native/` followed by a
# negation would leave the wasm unstageable.
packages/core/dist-native/*
!packages/core/dist-native/vector-scan.wasm
# The deployment's native tier, cross-compiled and committed for the same
# reason: Railway's build has no C toolchain. Named for its triple so it cannot
# shadow a Linux developer's own `vector-scan.so`, and built with -mavx2, which
# the container's AMD EPYC 9655P reports on every core.
!packages/core/dist-native/vector-scan-x86_64-linux-gnu.so
